# Purchase sheet

Completed Flute payments are upserted into a Google Sheet, one row per payment session. Human-facing notes live in `site-notes.md` under **Purchase sheet**. Keep that section in sync when this behavior changes.

## Contract

Tab `Purchases` (override with `GOOGLE_SHEETS_TAB`). Row 1 must equal `HEADERS` in `lib/purchase-sheet.js`:

`Timestamp | Email | Product | Product ID | Amount | Session ID`

`recordPurchase` reads `A:F`, finds the Flute session id in column F, and updates that row. Otherwise it appends. Missing `GOOGLE_SHEETS_SPREADSHEET_ID`, service-account email, or private key returns `{ recorded: false }` and does not throw. A configured write that fails throws.

Writers:

- `lib/webhooks.js` on `payment_session.completed`, after the online-course grant. A thrown sheet error returns HTTP 500 so Flute retries.
- `grantPaidCheckout` in `lib/checkout.js` for every paid session, including seminars and retreats. A sheet error is logged and must not block the access cookie.

Amount and product name come from `server/config/products.js`. An unknown product id is still written, with that id in both Product and Product ID. Emails are normalized. Do not record CSV grants from `scripts/grant-access.js` here.

## Changing the sheet

A new catalog product needs a `server/config/products.js` entry and a checkout link. Do not change the sheet or `lib/purchase-sheet.js` for that.

Changing columns means all of these in the same change:

1. `HEADERS` in `lib/purchase-sheet.js`
2. Row 1 of the live sheet, same names and order
3. The column table in `site-notes.md` and the header line in `flute-checkout.md`
4. Tests in `test/access.test.js` and `test/webhooks.test.js`

Writes refuse to run while the live header and `HEADERS` differ. Do not insert, rename, or reorder columns A–F. Columns after F are not written and can hold hand-written notes.

Env var changes (`GOOGLE_SHEETS_SPREADSHEET_ID`, `GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL`, `GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PRIVATE_KEY`, `GOOGLE_SHEETS_TAB`) apply on the next Vercel deployment. Never commit the private key.
